Statement-compression experiment -- LAS parameter set (n=6, ell=5, kappa=49, d=256)
100 iterations per candidate; fresh keys, statement and message each time.
Statement Y = 4416 B on the wire, against a 6736 B signature.

=== C0 -- CONTROL: the statement in full ===
  Y transmitted as STATEMENT_BYTES of 23-bit canonical packing: the scheme
  exactly as it stands. Every row must read `holds`, otherwise nothing below
  is attributable to compression.

  P1 PreVerify accepts the pre-signature          100 / 100   holds
  P2 Adapt succeeds                               100 / 100   holds
  P3 base Verify ACCEPTS the adapted signature  <-- the chain  100 / 100   holds
  P4 Ext recovers the exact witness             <-- atomicity  100 / 100   holds
  P5 adapted ||z||inf < BOUND_SIGN                100 / 100   holds

=== C2 -- SEED: send the 32-byte generator seed instead of Y ===
  The receiver reruns Gen and recomputes Y = A r' itself. Y is reproduced
  EXACTLY, so this is not an approximation and every functional property is
  expected to hold. It is also the largest compression available.

  P1 PreVerify accepts the pre-signature          100 / 100   holds
  P2 Adapt succeeds                               100 / 100   holds
  P3 base Verify ACCEPTS the adapted signature  <-- the chain  100 / 100   holds
  P4 Ext recovers the exact witness             <-- atomicity  100 / 100   holds
  P5 adapted ||z||inf < BOUND_SIGN                100 / 100   holds

  size            : 4416 B -> 32 B  (138x smaller)
  witness recovered by the receiver : 100 / 100

  VERDICT: inadmissible, and the row above is the demonstration rather
  than an argument. Gen derives BOTH Y and r' from this one seed, so the
  compressed statement IS the witness. A counterparty who can recompute Y
  can adapt the pre-signature and take both sides of the swap. The
  compression is total and so is the break.


########  C1 -- TRUNCATE: drop the b low bits of every coefficient  ########

This is precisely the move that shrinks ML-DSA's public key t to t1. Both
parties use the truncated statement, so the compression is consistent and
the hash agrees on both sides -- the fairest version of the idea.

=== C1(b=1) -- statement truncated to 22 bits/coefficient ===
  Statement 4416 B -> 4224 B (saves 192 B, 4%), applied identically at
  PreSign, PreVerify, Adapt and Ext.

  P1 PreVerify accepts the pre-signature          100 / 100   holds
  P2 Adapt succeeds                               100 / 100   holds
  P3 base Verify ACCEPTS the adapted signature  <-- the chain    0 / 100   FAILS
  P4 Ext recovers the exact witness             <-- atomicity    0 / 100   FAILS
  P5 adapted ||z||inf < BOUND_SIGN                100 / 100   holds
  coefficients actually moved by truncation : 76798 / 153600

