=== What this shows ===
Read the P3 and P4 rows of the C1 sweep against their P1 and P2 rows.

Truncation is INVISIBLE to the adaptor's own functions and FATAL to the two
that connect it to the outside world. PreSign and PreVerify both hash the
statement they were given, so they agree with each other whatever that
statement is; Adapt only re-runs PreVerify before adding the witness. But:

  (a) P3 fails because base Verify never sees a statement. It recomputes
      A z - c t, which for an adapted signature equals w + Y with the TRUE
      Y -- since A r' = Y exactly -- and hashes that. The pre-signature
      committed to w + Y_truncated. The two digests differ, so an unmodified
      verifier rejects. Nothing on the chain can be told about the
      compression, because the compression is not in the algebra.
  (b) P4 fails because Ext's acceptance test IS the exact relation: it
      returns s = z - z_hat only if A s == Y, and A s == Y_true, never
      Y_truncated. Atomicity is exactly this test, so a swap built on a
      truncated statement cannot complete its second leg.

The obstruction is not the norm budget and not the packing. It is that Y
enters the verification identity BEFORE any rounding, while ML-DSA's t
enters after -- which is what makes t compressible and Y not.

=== Why a hint cannot rescue the truncation ===
ML-DSA compresses t because the VERIFIER INDEPENDENTLY RECOMPUTES an
approximation of the rounded quantity, and the hint only has to repair the
last carry. No party can compute anything near Y without the witness --
that is the hardness assumption the statement rests on -- so a hint has no
approximation to correct and must instead carry the dropped bits outright:

  b=1    truncation saves    192 B   exact repair costs    192 B   net +0 B
  b=2    truncation saves    384 B   exact repair costs    384 B   net +0 B
  b=4    truncation saves    768 B   exact repair costs    768 B   net +0 B
  b=8    truncation saves   1536 B   exact repair costs   1536 B   net +0 B
  b=13   truncation saves   2496 B   exact repair costs   2496 B   net +0 B

The accounting is net zero BY CONSTRUCTION, not by coincidence: the bits a
hint would have to carry are exactly the bits truncation dropped.

The remaining escape -- sample r' so that A r' already has b zero low bits,
making the truncation lossless -- costs a factor 2^(b*n*d) in rejections:
  b=1 is 2^-1536 per attempt.

=== The compression already applied (the baseline to beat) ===
  one int32 per coefficient : 6144 B
  23-bit canonical packing  : 4416 B   (-28%, and lossless)

Y is 1536 coefficients that are uniform in [0,Q) under the MLWE assumption
the statement rests on, so 23 bits each is its entropy, not its encoding
overhead. A lossless encoder cannot do better; the C1 sweep is what happens
to a lossy one, and C2 is what happens when the loss is moved into the seed.

SCOPE: this harness settles FUNCTIONAL admissibility only. It shows which
compressions break the adaptor contract and where. It does not analyse the
security of any compression that survives -- no such candidate is reported
here, and security analysis is out of scope for the project.
